==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From Latin ''[[constitutum]]'', past participle of [[constituo|constituere]]. Constructed from the prefix [[con-]] and {{m|la|statuere||to place, set}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈkɒnstɪtjuːt/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈkɑnstɪt(j)uːt/|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|constitut}}

# To cause to stand; to [[establish]]; to [[enact]].
#* Jeremy Taylor
#*: Laws appointed and '''constituted''' by lawful authority.
# To [[make up]]; to [[compose]]; to [[form]].
#* Johnson
#*: Truth and reason '''constitute''' that intellectual gold that defies destruction.
# To [[appoint]], depute, or elect to an office; to make and empower.
#* William Wordsworth
#*: Me didst Thou '''constitute''' a priest of thine.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|establish}}, {{l|en|enact}}
* {{l|en|make up}}, {{l|en|compose}}, {{l|en|form}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|constituent}}
* {{l|en|constituency}}
* {{l|en|constitution}}
* {{l|en|constitutional}}
* {{l|en|constitutionalization}}
* {{l|en|constitutive}}

